// The Windows look's edit chrome: v3's TcxMaskEdit / TcxTextEdit / TcxSpinEdit / TcxButtonEdit frame, ellipsis button
// and spin buttons as the native Windows 11 theme draws them at 96 DPI, painted pixel for pixel from the captures in
// docs/ui/looks/ref/windows/states (rest, keyboard focus, pressed ellipsis, disabled). See scripts/looks_winedit_gen.py
// for how the tables are built and what the captures show: an edit of DFM width W is W + 1 pixels wide and 22 high, its
// top two rows and three foot rows drift linearly with the control's y in the window, and the left rim and the right end
// (rim, ellipsis or spin buttons) are fixed pixel blocks.
//
// The chrome is one custom node laid over the edit's white face; it paints only the pixels that are not plain white face
// (a tile cell of 0 is not painted), so the text, caret and selection under it stay as the editor draws them.

use e.gfx.geometry
use e.gfx.paint
use e.gfx.scene
use e.mem
use e.ui.layout as ui_layout
use e.ui.style
use e.ui.widget

// kind: 0 plain, 1 spin with fast buttons, 2 spin with stacked arrows, 3 button edit. state: 0 rest, 1 focused,
// 2 disabled, 3 ellipsis pressed (kind 3 only). `width` is the DFM width W; `window_y` the control's y in the v3 window
// (the foot's drift), negative for "where the node is laid out". `dim` bit i greys the i-th arrow of a spin edit.
type Chrome = struct { kind: u32, state: u32, width: f32, height: f32, window_y: f32, dim: u32 }

